#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096538 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096731 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f354f758-3745-5cee-9a4b-a0878c2c8578" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "85879186-0866-5159-905b-65d043681231" :question/math-academy-id "e-604" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e933e484b931ee54b345a8e7af3b0e44f80a8b019e3e401007ad4987192e33be.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = t^2 - 1, \\qquad y = t^3 - 4t$\nand the $x$-axis for $t \\in [-2,0]$." :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad y = y(t), \\qquad t_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - 1) \\\\\n&= 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}y\\frac{dx}{dt}dt &= \\int_{-2}^{0}(t^{3} - 4t)(2t)dt \\\\\n&= 2\\int_{-2}^{0}t^{2}(t^{2} - 4)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since\n\n$$\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\leq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $t \\in [-2,0]$.\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= -2\\int_{-2}^{0}t^{2}(t^{2} - 4)dt \\\\\n&= 2\\int_{-2}^{0}t^{2}(4 - t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= 2\\int_{-2}^{0}(4t^{2} - t^{4})dt \\\\\n&= 8\\int_{-2}^{0}t^{2}dt - 2\\int_{-2}^{0}t^{4}dt \\\\\n&= 8 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{t^{3}}{3}\\right|_{-2}^{0} - 2 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{t^{5}}{5}\\right|_{-2}^{0} \\\\\n&= 8(\\frac{0^{3}}{3} - ((-2)^{3})/(3)) - 2(\\frac{0^{5}}{5} - ((-2)^{5})/(5)) \\\\\n&= 8(\\frac{8}{3}) - 2(\\frac{32}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{64}{3} - \\frac{64}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{128}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096561 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20da72e-5a93-5a4e-be39-d412bd8fee86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "95cdc31d-c3f2-50b6-9447-b7317af775f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7412" :question/problem "![](images/3d/3dd84b2e84056a4e6c71f7b76534f5a6f1a46132ea5da5ed8edb2a514b9ea6b4.png)\n\nFind an integral expression for the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = e^{-t^2}, \\qquad y = t^3$,\nand the $x$-axis for $0 \\leq t \\leq \\sqrt{2} \\,$?" :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad y = y(t), \\qquad t_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{-t^{2}}) \\\\\n&=-2te^{-t^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}y\\frac{dx}{dt}dt &= \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t^{3}(-2te^{-t^{2}})dt \\\\\n&=-2\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t^{4}e^{-t^{2}}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since\n\n$$\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\leq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $t \\in [0, \\sqrt{2}]$.\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA = -\\left(-2 \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} t^4 e^{-t^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t \\right) = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} t^4 e^{-t^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20da72e-5a93-5a4e-be39-d412bd8fee86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eada6d87-e15d-59b5-9b10-60f77bcee43f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}(t^{3} - 1)(t^{2} - t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094772} {:answer/id #uuid "fc88212e-1ae9-522e-8d83-f5da9c66fb29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}(t^{3} - t)(t^{2} - t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094773} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc5dc3a-89b2-5658-8363-a30bfa05702d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t(t^{3} - t^{2})(t^{2} - 1)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094774} {:answer/id #uuid "46abf8b7-761a-5309-87bb-491ad91f0611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t(t^{3} - 1)(t^{2} - 1)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094775} {:answer/id #uuid "4b055023-d423-55ec-8127-24ff67ebbfa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}(t^{3} - t)(t^{2} - 1)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094776}] :answer-field/id #uuid "eeb17fdb-d008-5d2a-befe-3afdce935947" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094771}] :question/id #uuid "ad65213f-7896-5eb3-8900-a92c0319ea40" :question/math-academy-id "q-15688" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3bde5b93aa28654977fdb55b0b2808fde2fb2f54ba33a6e9a2a4281b6966682f.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = t^{4} - 2t^{2}, y = t^{3} - 1$\nand the $x$-axis for $t ∈ [1, \\sqrt{2}]$?"}]}
